    slug: "crematorium-design-requirements",
    title: "What Are the Design Requirements for a Crematorium?",
    description: "Crematorium design requirements cover retort exhaust stacks, heat management, air-quality permitting, and separation from viewing areas. What the engineering design must address before construction.",
    h1: "What Are the Design Requirements for a Crematorium?",
    answer: "The direct answer is that a crematorium is designed as an industrial thermal process housed inside a sensitive public building: the retort (cremation chamber) needs a properly engineered exhaust stack with emissions controls, the building needs heat and noise isolation between the equipment room and the chapel and viewing areas, and the project needs air-quality and health-department approvals before it can operate. The engineering succeeds when the family in the chapel never sees, hears, or smells the process happening down the hall.\n\nThe retort drives the mechanical design. Each retort has manufacturer-specified requirements for combustion air, exhaust flue sizing, and stack configuration, and I design the exhaust system from those requirements plus the applicable air-quality rules: stack height for proper dispersion, opacity and particulate controls, and afterburner or secondary-chamber provisions where the equipment or the permit requires them. Combustion air is supplied directly to the retort room so the equipment never starves the building's other systems of air, and the retort room gets its own ventilation and cooling — the heat rejected around the equipment is substantial, and the room has to stay workable for operators.\n\nHeat management extends beyond the equipment room. I evaluate heat-recovery opportunities — retort exhaust carries significant thermal energy that can preheat building hot water — but I design recovery as an optional system with proper isolation, because tying building systems too tightly to process equipment creates maintenance and reliability headaches. Noise and vibration isolation between the retort room and public spaces is designed in, not added later: mass, separation, and resilient mounting specified on the drawings. Viewing rooms, where families may witness the start of the process, get their own HVAC zone, dignified finishes, and sight-line control so the experience is calm and private.\n\nPermitting is where crematorium projects live or die. Air-quality approval — typically a permit from the state or regional air district covering emissions, stack parameters, and operating conditions — is the long-lead item, and its requirements shape the exhaust design. The health department, the building department, and zoning all have their own reviews, and the fire marshal reviews fuel-gas and fire-protection provisions. Jurisdiction matters: in many areas, air-quality permitting runs through a county or regional air district even when the building permit comes from the city, so the project answers to two different AHJs on parallel tracks. I identify both paths at the start and design the exhaust system to satisfy the stricter of the overlapping requirements.",
