        body: "I start the HVAC design from the volume and the surface. The air-distribution strategy delivers conditioned air to the playing and spectator zones — typically from high-bay ductwork or ductless high-volume units — while destratification fans keep the temperature profile uniform from floor to ceiling. In heating mode, those fans are the difference between comfortable courts and a building that burns energy heating empty air at the roof deck.\n\nDehumidification capacity is sized to the moisture load: occupants, infiltration through big doors, and any wet processes like ice resurfacing or turf watering. For hardwood, I hold the humidity band tight and design the controls with alarms on drift, because a failed humidistat can damage a court surface in a single season. For turf, the emphasis shifts to ventilation rate and temperature control. Either way, the big overhead doors get vestibules or air curtains — every door cycle in a fieldhouse is a massive slug of outside air, and the HVAC has to recover from it without drama.",
