    h1: "What Are the Design Requirements for a Police Station?",
    answer: "The direct answer is that a police station is designed as a secure operations building that never goes dark: a sally port controls every vehicle entering the secure side, evidence storage holds strict climate conditions with full access control, detention and holding areas use ligature-resistant, tamper-proof MEP, and emergency power keeps dispatch, communications, and security systems running through any outage. Security and continuity shape every system in the building.\n\nThe sally port is the secure vehicle entry — an enclosed, interlocked vestibule where a transport vehicle enters, the outer door closes, and only then does the inner door open. I design it with vehicle exhaust ventilation for the enclosed space, interlocked door controls coordinated with the security system, and drainage for washdown. The sally port connects to the detention intake and holding areas through a secure corridor, and the entire secure side of the building is laid out so the public, detainees, and staff circulate without crossing paths — a fundamental planning discipline the MEP design follows.\n\nEvidence storage is a climate-controlled vault with a chain of custody. I design it with tight temperature and humidity control — drugs, DNA, and biological evidence degrade without it — plus segregated zones for firearms, narcotics, currency, and general property, each with appropriate security and, where needed, refrigeration. Access control, intrusion detection, and video coverage are coordinated with the electrical and communications design, and the HVAC for evidence areas runs on emergency power so a prolonged outage cannot destroy the integrity of stored evidence. The property room's environmental monitoring gets alarming, because evidence compromised by a failed air conditioner is evidence lost.\n\nDetention and holding areas demand tamper-proof, ligature-resistant MEP: plumbing fixtures, diffusers, and lighting designed so nothing can be used for self-harm or as a weapon, ventilation that cannot be blocked from inside, and finishes that withstand abuse. Emergency power is sized for the mission — dispatch and 911, radio systems, security and access control, detention-area ventilation and lighting, and the data systems that cannot go down. Many jurisdictions add their own detention standards on top of the building code, and in unincorporated county areas the sheriff's facility may answer to county or state detention standards with the county as AHJ. I identify every applicable standard during design so the holding areas comply on day one, not after a costly retrofit.",
