    description: "Craft distillery MEP design manages ethanol vapor ventilation, classified electrical areas, barrel storage fire protection, and still heat loads. How engineers design a safe, compliant distillery.",
    h1: "How Is MEP Designed for a Craft Distillery?",
    answer: "The direct answer is that a craft distillery is designed as a flammable-liquid process: ethanol vapors are ventilated and monitored so they can never reach ignitable concentrations, electrical systems in process areas are classified for hazardous locations, barrel storage gets high-hazard fire protection, and the still's heat is managed as a major building load. The romance is in the whiskey; the engineering is in making sure the building never becomes the story.\n\nEthanol vapor management starts with the process layout. Stills, condensers, and transfer operations release ethanol vapors, and I design the ventilation to dilute and remove them continuously — general ventilation sized to the vapor load plus local exhaust at the points where vapors are generated, with the air-change strategy keeping concentrations far below ignitable levels under all operating conditions. Vapor detection with alarms backs up the ventilation design, and spill containment — curbed, drained, separated areas — keeps a liquid release from becoming a vapor event that travels. The still house is designed so vapors have a clear path out and no path to an ignition source.\n\nElectrical classification is the discipline most owners have never encountered. Areas around stills, spirit storage, and transfer operations are classified as hazardous locations, which means the electrical design uses rated enclosures, sealed conduit systems, and fixtures approved for the classification — no standard commercial electrical in those zones. I define the classified-area boundaries from the process layout and the applicable standards, and I carry the classification through every related system: lighting, power, controls, and even the building automation devices in the space. Getting the boundaries right matters — over-classifying wastes money on every device, under-classifying creates the hazard the design exists to prevent.\n\nBarrel storage and still heat complete the design. Barrel warehouses hold enormous quantities of high-proof spirits in wood — a high-hazard fire load — so I design the fire protection for it: sprinkler protection designed to the high-hazard commodity, with the water supply, drainage for suppression discharge, and fire-marshal coordination the hazard demands. The still itself rejects major heat into the still house, so the HVAC design handles that load while maintaining the ventilation strategy — cooling without short-circuiting the vapor-dilution airflow. And the regulatory stack is tall: the federal TTB permit for distilled spirits, the state liquor authority, the local fire marshal for flammable liquids and hazardous processes, the building and health departments, and the sewer authority for high-strength process wastewater. I sequence the design with that full AHJ list in view, because the fire marshal's hazardous-materials review and the TTB timeline both reward early, complete submittals.",
    directAnswer: "A craft distillery is designed around ethanol vapor ventilation and detection, classified electrical systems in hazardous areas, high-hazard fire protection for barrel storage, still-heat management, and a multi-agency permit path including TTB, the fire marshal, and the state liquor authority.",
    topic: "Building-Type Engineering Guides",
